// W Linux — let an active local session abort a fingerprint verification.
//
// The W auth card's "Cancel" and "Use password" must stop the reader NOW. Nothing
// can interrupt pam_fprintd from outside: since polkit 126 the PAM conversation
// runs in polkit-agent-helper@.service — a socket-activated ROOT unit — so
// PolkitAgent.Session.cancel() has no child process left to signal, and the module
// keeps the sensor claimed until its own timeout (30s) runs out. Measured on real
// hardware: the card closed in 1 ms, the reader stayed lit for the remaining 27 s,
// and the next prompt could not use a fingerprint at all because the device was
// still claimed by the abandoned conversation.
//
// The one abort path pam_fprintd itself honours is fprintd leaving the bus: its
// NameOwnerChanged handler returns PAM_AUTHINFO_UNAVAIL immediately, without
// spending one of max-tries ("give up as we might start listening to events from a
// new name owner otherwise"). So w-authd restarts fprintd.service, and this rule is
// what lets it do so — without raising a second authentication from inside the
// handler of the first one. w-authd calls RestartUnit non-interactively, so if this
// rule is ever missing or broken, polkitd simply refuses instead of prompting.
//
// Scope: this ONE unit, this ONE verb, and only for a session that is local and
// active. It grants no new power over the reader — any local user can already
// occupy it with a plain Claim (that is what a fingerprint login does), so denying
// the scanner to others was always possible; this only makes RELEASING it possible
// too. See quickshell-auth.md.
polkit.addRule(function (action, subject) {
    if (action.id == "org.freedesktop.systemd1.manage-units" &&
        action.lookup("unit") == "fprintd.service" &&
        action.lookup("verb") == "restart" &&
        subject.local && subject.active) {
        return polkit.Result.YES;
    }
});
